<spell-notebook "detail usage">
	<header level="1">
		Detail
		<header>A detail is a label's dimmer second value, such as a count.</>
	</>
	<detail-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#owners">Owners</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-detail>` is a label's dimmer second value, such as a count: Fomantic's `.detail` inside a `ui label`.

			- Slot it into a `<ui-label>`, after the label's text.
			- `link` renders it as a link of its own (`<a class="detail">`), apart from the label.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Owners">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-detail>` finds its nearest owner and sets `:state(in-<owner>)` on itself; `UIParts.css` styles it by that
			state. Its owners, and what each makes of it:

			- [Label](/components/ui/ui-label.html) (`:state(in-label)`): dimmer and bold, after the label's text

			Anywhere else it is an unstyled block. More on how parts find their owners: [Content parts](/components/ui/ui-header.html).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Read together with the label's text ("Dogs 214"): make the pair make sense out loud, or give the label an
			  `aria-label`.
			- With `link` it is a real `<a>`: **Tab** reaches it, **Enter** follows it.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
